HOME_ROADMAP_15 = [
("solar-panels-worth-it-2026", "understand",
"Are solar panels still worth it in 2026? The honest maths",
"Payback now runs about a decade on average and is driven by your electricity rate, not your sunshine - and the US federal credit is gone. What to check before you sign.",
"""<p>Solar went from green gesture to genuine home economics and back to a subtler question. The honest answer for 2026: it can still be worth it - but the case now depends almost entirely on your specific house and, above all, your local electricity rate.</p>
<h2>What changed</h2>
<p>For years, the US federal tax credit did heavy lifting in the payback maths. That credit expired at the end of 2025, and quotes that still quietly assume it are a red flag. Without it, US payback periods average around a decade, with a wide spread - roughly five years in favourable states to eighteen-plus in poor ones. The single biggest driver is not how sunny your roof is; it is what you pay per kilowatt-hour. High-rate regions make every generated unit valuable; cheap-power regions make solar a slow investment.</p>
<h2>The five questions that decide it for your house</h2>
<p><b>1. Your rate and your usage.</b> Pull a year of bills. Expensive power plus predictable daytime consumption is the ideal customer; cheap power plus mostly-empty daytime house is the hardest case.</p>
<p><b>2. Your roof.</b> Age and condition matter more than angle: panels last twenty-five-plus years, so a roof needing replacement in five means paying twice. Shading from trees and neighbouring buildings trims output all day, every day.</p>
<p><b>3. Your local rules.</b> Whether your utility offers net metering - crediting you for power you export - swings the maths enormously. Check it before any quote.</p>
<p><b>4. Buy or lease.</b> Buying (cash or loan) keeps the savings; leases and power-purchase agreements trade a smaller bill drop for zero upfront cost and can complicate a future house sale. Read who owns the system and who claims any incentives.</p>
<p><b>5. The quote's honesty.</b> Get three quotes, demand the payback maths in writing with the assumptions shown, and be suspicious of door-to-door urgency and "prices rising next month" pressure - the panel market is not that fragile.</p>
<h2>Where solar clearly still shines</h2>
<p>High-rate regions with good sun, homes with daytime occupancy or charging an electric car, and places pairing panels with a battery where outages or time-of-use pricing make storage pay. Elsewhere, the honest move is the boring one first: <a href="/home/standby-power-real-numbers-your-meter/">measuring and cutting waste</a> and <a href="/home/smart-thermostat-payback/">fixing heating control</a> costs hundreds, not thousands, and every unit you never use improves any future solar maths - <a href="/home/what-size-generator-do-i-need/">and if your real problem is outages rather than bills, size the backup properly instead</a>.</p>"""),

("hvac-filter-sizes-and-merv", "maintain",
"HVAC filter sizes and MERV ratings, decoded (and the mistake that strains your system)",
"How to read the number printed on the frame, what MERV actually rates, and why the 'best' filter can be the wrong filter.",
"""<p>The cardboard-framed filter slot is the most misunderstood part of a central heating and cooling system. Two numbers run it - size and MERV - and getting both right takes two minutes.</p>
<h2>Reading the size (it is printed on the frame)</h2>
<p>Something like 20x25x1: length x width x depth in inches. Two traps live here. First, the printed size is often the nominal one - the actual piece may be a half-inch smaller on each side, so filters are sold as "20x25x1" while actually measuring 19.5x24.5. Buy the number printed on your old filter, not a ruler reading. Second, depth matters: if your slot takes a 4-inch-deep media filter, the thin 1-inch versions are not a substitute - the system was designed for one or the other.</p>
<p>No filter to read? Measure the slot's opening and depth with the system off, and when in doubt, a slightly-too-tall fit sealed around the edge beats a gap at the side - air takes the shortcut around any loose filter, and unfiltered dust is what coats the blower.</p>
<h2>MERV: what the rating means</h2>
<p>MERV rates how much the filter catches at which particle sizes, on a scale from 1 to 16 for ordinary homes. Higher is more filtration - and more resistance to airflow. The honest guidance: MERV 8 to 11 keeps household dust and pollen down while breathing easily, which is the sweet spot for most systems. MERV 13 catches finer particles, including some smoke - genuinely worth it for wildfire-smoke seasons or allergy households, <b>but only if your system is rated to push air through it</b>. A filter deeper than the blower was designed for makes the fan strain: higher bills, weaker rooms, and in the worst case a frozen coil or early fan death. Your system's manual (or a quick question to an HVAC technician) settles what it can handle - this is also why "highest MERV available" is not automatically the best filter.</p>
<h2>The rhythm that matters more than the rating</h2>
<p>A clogged high-MERV filter restricts airflow worse than a fresh one of any rating. Check monthly, replace when grey and loaded - typically every one to three months, more often with pets - <a href="/home/hvac-filter-change-habit/">here is the habit that makes it automatic</a>. Write the install date on the frame edge in pencil; future-you will not remember. And if some rooms run weak while others roar, the filter is suspect number one - <a href="/home/seasonal-home-maintenance-checklist/">fold the check into the seasonal routine</a> alongside <a href="/home/condensation-ventilation-that-works/">the ventilation basics</a>.</p>"""),

("water-heater-flush-how-to", "maintain",
"How to flush a water heater (and why the rumbling matters)",
"Sediment is the crackle and pop in the tank. The once-a-year drain that quiets it, the safety steps that come first, and the line where a pro takes over.",
"""<p>A water heater that crackles or rumbles while heating is telling you about sediment: minerals settled on the tank floor, trapping water that pops and steams as it tries to escape. Left alone for years, the layer insulates the burner from the water - longer heating, higher bills, and a shorter tank life. An annual flush is the standard, boring fix.</p>
<h2>Before anything: the two shut-offs</h2>
<p><b>Electric heater:</b> switch it off at the breaker first - heating elements running dry burn out in minutes, and live elements plus draining water is a combination you never want. <b>Gas heater:</b> set the gas control to PILOT (or off per the manual) so the burner cannot fire while the tank drains. Cold-water inlet: closed. Then give hot water a head start by running hot at any tap until it runs warm - you will be gentler on your arms later.</p>
<h2>The flush, in five honest steps</h2>
<p><b>1.</b> Connect a garden hose to the drain valve at the tank's base and run it outside or to a utility sink - the water will be hot, so the hose end needs securing.</p>
<p><b>2.</b> Open the drain valve, then open a hot tap somewhere in the house (or the pressure-relief lever per the manual) so air can replace the leaving water.</p>
<p><b>3.</b> Let it run until the water flows clear and cool-ish - minutes if flushed yearly, longer and grittier if this is its first ever flush.</p>
<p><b>4.</b> For a deeper clean, briefly reopen the cold inlet a couple of times while draining to stir the settled layer.</p>
<p><b>5.</b> Close the drain, close the hot tap you opened, reopen the cold inlet and let the tank fill completely - confirm hot runs full and steady at that tap again - <i>then</i> restore power or gas. Heating an empty tank is the classic self-inflicted kill.</p>
<h2>When to call a professional instead</h2>
<p>If the drain valve is fused solid or drips after closing, if the tank is more than a decade old and has never been flushed (old tanks occasionally leak once disturbed - better discovered by a pro), or if the pressure-relief valve weeps afterwards. A pro visit every few years should also include the sacrificial anode rod check - the part that dissolves so the tank does not.</p>
<p>Flushed yearly, a tank routinely outlives its warranty by years; neglected, it rusts quietly toward the day it fails on a weekend. Pair the habit with <a href="/home/water-heater-explained/">knowing how your heater works</a>, <a href="/home/how-to-shut-off-water-main/">finding your water main shutoff before an emergency</a>, and <a href="/home/basic-toolkit-checklist/">the small toolkit this job needs</a>.</p>"""),

("dryer-vent-cleaning-fire-risk", "maintain",
"The dryer vent: the annual clean that prevents a house fire",
"Lint escapes the trap and packs the duct. The warning signs, the safe clean-out, and the habits fire investigators wish everyone had.",
"""<p>Every load sheds lint. The trap catches most; the rest travels into the duct and settles there, packing year by year into a fuzzy, heat-hardened lining - and it is exactly the fuel and exactly the location fire-safety agencies name when they list "failure to clean" as the leading factor in dryer fires.</p>
<h2>The warning signs your vent is loading up</h2>
<p><b>Longer dry times</b> - the first and most-ignored symptom: the machine is fine, the exhaust is choked. <b>Laundry hot to the touch</b> at cycle's end, the dryer itself running hotter than usual. <b>The outside vent flap barely flutters</b> when the dryer runs. <b>A musty or burning smell</b> - the burning smell means stop the dryer immediately and investigate, not next cycle.</p>
<h2>The clean-out, step by step</h2>
<p><b>1.</b> Unplug the dryer (or switch its breaker off); gas dryers also get the gas valve closed.</p>
<p><b>2.</b> Pull the machine forward and detach the duct from the back - usually a simple clamp.</p>
<p><b>3.</b> Clear the hood exit outside: open the flap, remove the visible lint, confirm the flap swings freely - snow, nests and paint seals it shut.</p>
<p><b>4.</b> Clean the duct: a vent-brush kit on a drill spirals the length of the duct and pulls out the packed lining; a leaf blower or vacuum is a rough second. You want daylight-and-air from one end to the other.</p>
<p><b>5.</b> Reattach, push the dryer back leaving the duct not-kinked (crushed flexible duct is its own airflow killer), and run an empty cycle to confirm strong airflow outside.</p>
<p>Once a year for a short straight duct; every six months for long runs or big households. Call a pro if the duct runs through walls and ceilings you cannot reach, or if a kit cannot clear it.</p>
<h2>The habits that carry most of the protection</h2>
<p>Clean the lint trap every single load - no exceptions. Never run the dryer asleep or out of the house; fires that start unattended start bad. Plastic or foil ducting is the old standard and the worst material - replace with rigid or semi-rigid metal. And keep the area around the machine free of stored boxes and solvents. Ten minutes a year is the whole price of removing one of the home's quietest fire risks - <a href="/home/dryer-lint-every-load/">the trap habit is here</a>, and the same fire-becomes-real logic applies to <a href="/home/electrical-fire-warning-signs/">the warning signs in wiring</a> and <a href="/home/burning-plastic-smell-socket/">a socket that smells wrong</a>.</p>"""),

("smart-home-devices-worth-it", "understand",
"Smart home devices: the ones worth it, the ones that aren't",
"Start with problems, not gadgets: the four devices that earn their plug, the category to skip, and the privacy defaults to change.",
"""<p>The smart home market sells the future by the gadget. Most of it is a phone screen standing in for a switch you already owned. A handful of devices, though, genuinely earn their plug - and the difference between the two groups is a question: does this solve a problem my house actually has?</p>
<h2>Worth it - the short list</h2>
<p><b>Smart thermostat.</b> The one with real money attached: heating and cooling are the home's biggest power users, and proper scheduling and away-detection shave genuine amounts off - the honest payback maths is <a href="/home/smart-thermostat-payback/">here</a>.</p>
<p><b>Water-leak sensors.</b> Twenty-ish currency units apiece, placed under the washing machine, water heater and under every sink. They beep the day a hose starts dripping instead of the month you find the ceiling stain. Per pound, the best insurance in the entire smart home aisle.</p>
<p><b>Video doorbell.</b> Real convenience and genuine deterrence, priced once. Buy the storage plan consciously - check its yearly fee before the purchase, not after.</p>
<p><b>Smart plugs.</b> For the specific job of killing standby drain on the media centre and the desk - <a href="/home/standby-power-real-numbers-your-meter/">the measured cost of always-on devices is here</a> - and for lamps on holiday schedules.</p>
<h2>Worth skipping - for now</h2>
<p><b>Smart fridges, washers and ovens.</b> The screen adds cost and a future software-obsolescence problem to machines whose job was to run for twenty years without updates - <a href="/home/smart-appliances-worth-it/">the appliance-by-appliance sorting is here</a>. <b>Voice-everything by default.</b> Start with the switch-shaped problems; the microphone can wait for an actual use.</p>
<h2>The five minutes of privacy setup</h2>
<p>Whatever you buy: change default passwords on day one, decline the data-sharing toggles during setup, turn on automatic updates, and name devices something other than your surname. A camera pointed at your street deserves a moment's thought about where its footage goes.</p>
<p>The pattern across all of it: buy for the leak, the bill, the package and the lock - not for the demo. One honest thermostat and two leak sensors do more for a house than a shelf of colour-changing bulbs - and they cost less together than any one of them cost three years ago.</p>"""),

("how-to-shut-off-water-main", "fix",
"Find and use your water main shutoff (before the emergency)",
"Every adult in the house should be able to kill the water in under a minute. The three places it hides, the right direction to turn, and the fixture-level backups.",
"""<p>The worst plumbing moments are not the leak itself - they are the twenty minutes of water still arriving while everyone searches for the stopcock. This is a five-minute preparation with a thousand-times-that payoff, and it starts today, not mid-emergency.</p>
<h2>The three places your shutoff hides</h2>
<p><b>UK:</b> the internal stopcock usually sits under the kitchen sink, in a downstairs cloakroom, or in the hall - a small valve on the rising main, often with a lever or a screwdown handle. Older houses may rely on an outside street stopcock under a metal cover in the pavement (you may need a proper key - check now, not flooded).</p>
<p><b>US:</b> the main shutoff is typically where the supply enters the house - basement or crawlspace front wall, garage, or a ground-floor utility wall - plus a curb box near the street. <b>AU/CA:</b> same logic - trace the incoming pipe from the water meter, which itself sits near the property line.</p>
<h2>The turn, and the test</h2>
<p>Gate-style valve (the round handle): clockwise until firm - "righty-tighty" closes water just like a jar. Lever ball valve: the lever sits <i>along</i> the pipe when open and swings <i>across</i> it when closed. Run the test now: turn it off, open the kitchen tap, confirm the flow dies to a spit, turn it back on, confirm full pressure returns. An untested shutoff is a rumour. If it turns but never fully stops water, it needs replacing - a cheap job <i>today</i>.</p>
<p>Tag it. A laminated card or taped note - "WATER MAIN" - turns a panic search into a point-and-turn for whoever is home when a hose bursts, including the teenager and the plumber's apprentice.</p>
<h2>The fixture-level backups (often faster)</h2>
<p>Most problems are local, and every wet appliance has its own valve: small handles behind the toilet, under every sink, and - the ones people never notice - on the washing machine hoses. Turning those kills a washer leak without darkening the whole house. While you are at it, eyeball those washer hoses: any bubbling or cracking means replacement with braided-steel ones, this weekend.</p>
<p>Then learn the two neighbours of the main: <a href="/home/hidden-water-leak-meter-test/">the meter trick that catches invisible leaks</a> and <a href="/home/how-to-fix-a-dripping-tap/">the tap repair every home eventually needs</a> - and if a pipe does burst, the first minute belongs to this valve; the second minute can belong to <a href="/home/washing-machine-wont-drain/">calm diagnosis instead of towels</a>.</p>"""),

("garage-door-spring-safety", "fix",
"Garage door springs: the one repair you never make yourself",
"The door's weight hangs on springs under violent tension - everything else is homeowner territory, and the line between the two is worth knowing precisely.",
"""<p>A garage door is heavy - often north of a hundred kilos - and it opens like a feather because steel springs store the door's full weight in torsion. That stored energy is also why the spring line is the single clearest do-not-DIY boundary in the entire house.</p>
<h2>Why springs are professional-only, full stop</h2>
<p>Torsion springs sit on a shaft above the door, wound tight to many hundreds of newton-metres. Winding or unwinding them without the proper bars and training has injured and killed experienced handymen - the bar becomes a lever the spring's energy controls, not you. There is no partial credit for trying: an unwound spring wrong, a slipped bar, a clamp that lets go - each failure mode is severe. Extension springs on older doors are similarly loaded. If a spring is broken or the door is suddenly heavy: stop using the opener, keep people clear, and call a door technician. This is also not a "get three quotes" repair to agonise over - spring replacement is routine, same-day work for them.</p>
<h2>How do you know the spring broke?</h2>
<p>A bang from the garage like something fell; the opener straining or clicking while the door rises a few centimetres and stops; the visible gap in a twisted spring on the shaft; or the door feeling absurdly heavy on manual lift. Do not keep pressing the opener - it will burn itself out fighting a door its springs no longer carry.</p>
<h2>Everything else is genuinely yours</h2>
<p><b>Rollers and hinges:</b> a few drops of lubricant (garage-door lube, not thick grease) on rollers, hinges and the opener's rail, once or twice a year, removes most squeaks and strains.</p>
<p><b>The auto-reverse test, twice a year:</b> with the door closing, wave something through the photo-eye beam near the floor - it must reverse. Then place a block of wood under the door and let it close - it must touch and reverse. If it fails either test, the door can close on a child or a car bonnet; the adjustment is in the opener manual and takes minutes.</p>
<p><b>Hardware check:</b> tighten loose hinge bolts, watch a full open-close cycle for any jerking or belt slipping, and replace weather seal when daylight shows under the door.</p>
<p>The pattern is the house-wide rule: <a href="/home/basic-toolkit-checklist/">the toolkit handles the mechanical</a>, and the line to a professional is drawn by stored energy and gas - the same instinct that keeps <a href="/home/electrical-fire-warning-signs/">wiring</a> and <a href="/home/outlet-overloading-danger/">overloaded circuits</a> honest. Springs sit firmly on the pro side of that line.</p>"""),

("gutter-guards-worth-it", "understand",
"Gutter guards: worth it or a plastic grin on a clogged system?",
"What guards actually stop, what still gets through, and the honest maths against a ladder and an hour twice a year.",
"""<p>Gutter guards promise the end of the ladder. The honest version: good guards turn a twice-a-year clean into a once-a-year check - they reduce the work, they do not delete it, and some types quietly create their own problems.</p>
<h2>What actually clogs gutters</h2>
<p>Leaves are the famous culprit; the quiet ones are the shingle grit that washes off roofs, seeds, pine needles and the helicopter seeds maples throw. Water needs the trough; the trough is horizontal and outside. Whatever your guard, water still arrives with the roof's full runoff - including everything small enough to ride along.</p>
<h2>The types, honestly</h2>
<p><b>Fine mesh (metal, screwed to the fascia):</b> the respectable choice - blocks leaves and needles, sheds grit into the flow... eventually some grit passes through and settles under it. Costs more; lasts.</p>
<p><b>Reverse-curve covers:</b> water wraps the curve, debris slides off - works in theory, and in heavy downpours some water simply shoots past the gutter entirely, delivering the rain to your foundation instead.</p>
<p><b>Brush and foam inserts:</b> the budget shelf - and the honest verdict is they sit <i>in</i> the gutter catching debris, where seeds sprout in the brush bristles and foam degrades in sunlight. They turn one cleaning job into a worse one.</p>
<h2>The maths that decides it</h2>
<p>A guard system costs real money per metre plus installation. The alternative is a check twice a year - after autumn's drop and once in spring - that takes an hour on a single-storey house. Two storeys, steep pitch, towering trees over the roofline, or knees-and-ladders that no longer agree: that is where guards genuinely pay, in money if not in ladders. A bungalow with one small tree is the case where the ladder wins for another decade.</p>
<h2>If you fit guards</h2>
<p>Clean the gutters properly first - sealing grit and moss <i>under</i> a guard is the classic self-inflicted clog. Choose screwed metal mesh over clip-on plastic, expect to check (not clean) annually, and confirm the downspouts still take water freely after the first big storm - <a href="/home/gutters-and-downpipes/">the gutter system itself is explained here</a>, and a guard is only as good as <a href="/home/flat-roof-ponding-and-leaks/">the drainage waiting below it</a>. Whatever you choose, the water's exit path matters more than its entrance - <a href="/home/seasonal-home-maintenance-checklist/">keep the check on the seasonal list either way</a>.</p>"""),
]
